    <h2>Context and Objectives</h2>
    <p>
      This project was developed to demonstrate a deep mastery of containerization, automation, and Fullstack development. The objective was to simulate a real VPS provider (like DigitalOcean, Scaleway, or OVH) capable of provisioning isolated, secure, and strictly resource-limited environments on demand, all from a unified web interface.
    </p>
    <p>
      Manual server management is tedious and error-prone. <strong>Mini Cloud Provider</strong> aims to automate this process while guaranteeing that resource isolation (via Linux <strong>Cgroups</strong> and <strong>Namespaces</strong>) protects the host machine, even in the event of a load spike or memory leak in a client container.
    </p>

    <h2>Technical Architecture</h2>
    <p>The system relies on a clear separation of concerns:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Frontend</strong>: Lightweight and reactive interface without heavy frameworks (Vanilla JS), communicating via Fetch API to minimize latency.</li>
      <li><strong>FastAPI Backend</strong>: Single entry point that validates data (Pydantic), manages state, and orchestrates Docker via the Python SDK.</li>
      <li><strong>Docker Engine</strong>: Manages isolation via namespaces (PID, NET, MNT) and limits hardware resources via cgroups.</li>
      <li><strong>Custom Base Image</strong>: Optimized Debian container with <code>ttyd</code> for immediate and authenticated web terminal access, avoiding the installation of a heavy SSH server.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Phase 1: Creating the Optimized Base Image</h2>
    <p>To avoid long installation times during each deployment (which would slow down the API), I created a dedicated, lightweight, ready-to-use Docker image:</p>
    <pre><code>{dockerfileSnippet}</code></pre>
    <p><em>Note: Using a <code>slim</code> image and cleaning the <code>apt</code> cache reduces the image size from ~150MB to ~40MB, significantly accelerating provisioning time.</em></p>

    <h2>Phase 2: Orchestration via FastAPI</h2>
    <p>The core of the system is the creation endpoint. It doesn't just launch a container; it strictly applies the resource limits requested by the user:</p>
    <pre><code>{pythonApiSnippet}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The <code>mem_limit</code> argument and the <code>cpu_quota</code>/<code>cpu_period</code> combinations are crucial. They instruct the Linux kernel to apply real hardware limits to the container. For example, a <code>cpu_quota</code> of 50000 on a <code>cpu_period</code> of 100000 means the container can only use 50% of a CPU core, preventing excessive consumption.
    </p>

    <h2>Phase 3: Persistent Storage Management</h2>
    <p>By default, Docker container data is ephemeral. To simulate a real VPS, each instance is assigned a named Docker volume (e.g., <code>vps-myproject-xxxx-data</code>), mounted on <code>/data</code> in read/write mode. This ensures that files created by the user survive container restarts or recreations.</p>

    <h2>Phase 4: Security and Network Access</h2>
    <p>Rather than exposing ports statically (which would cause conflicts if two VPS instances request port 80), the backend retrieves the dynamic port assigned by Docker (<code>HostPort</code>). It then generates a unique URL using the <code>nip.io</code> wildcard DNS resolution service (e.g., <code>myvps.127.0.0.1.nip.io:32768</code>). Terminal access is protected by HTTP Basic authentication enforced directly by the <code>ttyd</code> startup command (<code>-c 'user:pass'</code>).</p>

    <h2>Technical Challenges Encountered and Resolutions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Python Docker SDK Version Conflict:</strong> During development, a <code>URLSchemeUnknown: Not supported URL scheme http+docker</code> error occurred. <em>Resolution:</em> Forced update of the <code>docker>=7.0.0</code> dependency in <code>requirements.txt</code> to ensure compatibility with recent <code>urllib3</code> versions.</li>
      <li><strong>Docker Socket Mounting on Windows/WSL2:</strong> Traefik encountered permission errors with the <code>:ro</code> (read-only) flag on the socket mount. <em>Resolution:</em> Removed the <code>:ro</code> flag and explicitly defined the endpoint in the Traefik configuration to ensure full compatibility with the WSL2 architecture.</li>
    </ul>

