    <h2>Future Improvements</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Database</strong>: Replace the in-memory registry with PostgreSQL for metadata persistence and real multi-user management.</li>
      <li><strong>User Authentication</strong>: Client account system with JWT and strict VPS isolation by owner (RBAC).</li>
      <li><strong>Advanced Monitoring</strong>: Integration of Prometheus (via cAdvisor) and Grafana for historical metrics and alerting.</li>
      <li><strong>Cloud Deployment</strong>: Migration of the local host to an AWS EC2 or DigitalOcean server with provisioning via Terraform.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Appendix: Exhaustive Command Log</h2>
    <p>
      Here is the complete list of commands used, categorized by phase. Each command has a precise technical utility in the workflow.
    </p>

    <h3>1. Build and Orchestration (Host)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>docker build -t mini-cloud-provider_vps-base ./vps-base</code>: Builds the optimized image containing ttyd and base tools.</li>
      <li><code>docker-compose up --build</code>: Starts the complete ecosystem (Frontend, Backend, Network) applying the latest code modifications.</li>
      <li><code>docker stats</code>: <strong>Crucial command.</strong> Displays real-time CPU and RAM consumption. This is living proof that <code>cgroups</code> are working and limiting the VPS to its quota (e.g., 512m), even if the internal process tries to use more.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Usage and Validation (Inside the VPS Web Terminal)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>apt-get update && apt-get install -y python3</code>: Updates Debian repositories and installs the Python environment to run custom code.</li>
      <li><code>cat > /data/monitor.py << 'EOF' ... EOF</code>: Uses a "Here Document" to create a multi-line file directly in the terminal, without needing to open an interactive editor like nano or vim.</li>
      <li><code>python3 /data/monitor.py &</code>: Runs the Python script in the background (the <code>&</code> detaches the process from the current terminal, acting as a daemon).</li>
      <li><code>tail -f /data/logs/monitor.log</code>: Displays new lines of the log file in real time ("follow"). This is the #1 debugging tool to verify service activity without interrupting its execution.</li>
      <li><code>ps aux | grep python</code>: Lists running processes and filters to verify that our monitoring script is indeed running in the background with the correct PID.</li>
      <li><code>df -h /data</code>: Checks available and used disk space on the mounted persistent volume, confirming that data is written to dedicated storage and not the container's ephemeral layer.</li>
    </ul>

